{
  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "q10",
  "speechText": "Yes, I have hands-on experience with tool calling, especially on the backend control side. For example, in Clincove, the model can use read-only tools like search and document read. Then I would expose a small set of backend tools. The model can decide which tool it needs. But the backend still defines the tool contract, validates the arguments, and checks permission before doing it. For the interface, we design the tool interface in a familiar way for the LLM, like a file system with search and read, because this usually helps the model perform better. For tracking, I keep clear logs with the important IDs, status, errors, and performance metrics, such as latency, processing time, and memory usage. This helps me monitor the system, find what went wrong, and debug problems more easily. For tool safety, I also worked on audit logs, tracing, citations, and final-response validation. So overall, I directly worked on the Clincove tool-calling flow, and I also understand and can modify parts of the StrangeLoop engine when needed.",
